## Solution

For this challenge, I followed the approach in
the uploaded suggested solution.

The main idea is to create an ObjClosure only
when a function captures variables from its
enclosing scope.

In Chapter 25, the compiler already tracks the
number of upvalues a function uses through:

```c
function->upvalueCount
```

An upvalue represents a variable captured from
an enclosing function.

If upvalueCount is greater than zero, the
function needs a closure.

If upvalueCount is zero, the function can
potentially be used directly as an ObjFunction.

This avoids creating an extra closure object
for functions that do not capture anything.

However, it also makes the VM more complicated
because it must support two different types
of callable Lox functions.

## 1. Modify Function Compilation

In the original Chapter 25 implementation,
the compiler emits OP_CLOSURE for every
function declaration.

For this challenge, I would change the
function() compiler helper so that it
checks the function's upvalue count.

In compiler.c, replace the code that emits
the function object at the end of function()
with:

```c
ObjFunction* function = endCompiler();

uint8_t functionConstant =
    makeConstant(OBJ_VAL(function));

if (function->upvalueCount > 0) {
  // The function captures surrounding variables.
  emitBytes(OP_CLOSURE, functionConstant);

  // Describe each captured variable.
  for (int i = 0; i < function->upvalueCount; i++) {
    emitByte(compiler.upvalues[i].isLocal ? 1 : 0);
    emitByte(compiler.upvalues[i].index);
  }
} else {
  // No captured variables are needed.
  emitBytes(OP_CONSTANT, functionConstant);
}
```

This creates two possible outcomes.

If the function captures variables, the compiler
generates OP_CLOSURE followed by the information
needed to connect its upvalues.

If the function does not capture variables,
the compiler simply emits OP_CONSTANT.

The VM can then load and use the function
without allocating an ObjClosure.

## 2. Update the CallFrame Structure

In the original Chapter 25 implementation,
each CallFrame stores a pointer to an ObjClosure.

This works when all functions are wrapped,
but it will no longer work after our change.

A CallFrame might now represent either:

- An ObjFunction that has no captured variables.
- An ObjClosure that captures one or more variables.

To support both, I would update CallFrame
in vm.h:

```c
typedef struct {
  Obj* function;
  uint8_t* ip;
  Value* slots;
} CallFrame;
```

The function field now stores a pointer
to the common Obj object header.

Since Obj contains the object's type,
the VM can determine whether it is
working with an ordinary function
or a closure.

## 3. Add a Helper to Access the Function

The VM still needs to access the underlying
ObjFunction when retrieving bytecode,
constants, and function information.

Since a CallFrame may contain either
type of callable object, I would add
the following helper in vm.c:

```c
static inline ObjFunction* getFrameFunction(
    CallFrame* frame) {
  if (frame->function->type == OBJ_FUNCTION) {
    return (ObjFunction*)frame->function;
  } else {
    return ((ObjClosure*)frame->function)->function;
  }
}
```

If the CallFrame contains an ObjFunction,
the helper returns that function directly.

If the CallFrame contains an ObjClosure,
it retrieves the ObjFunction stored
inside the closure.

This adds a type check, but it also lets
other parts of the VM use the same helper
without duplicating the logic.

## 4. Update Function Calls

The VM's callValue() function must now
accept both types of callable objects.

In vm.c, update the relevant switch cases:

```c
case OBJ_CLOSURE:
  return callClosure(AS_CLOSURE(callee), argCount);

case OBJ_FUNCTION:
  return callFunction(AS_FUNCTION(callee), argCount);
```

The native function case from Chapter 24
should remain in callValue() as well.

Instead of maintaining two completely
separate implementations for ordinary
functions and closures, I would use a
shared helper.

```c
static bool call(Obj* callee,
                 ObjFunction* function,
                 int argCount) {
  if (argCount != function->arity) {
    runtimeError("Expected %d arguments but got %d.",
                 function->arity, argCount);
    return false;
  }

  if (vm.frameCount == FRAMES_MAX) {
    runtimeError("Stack overflow.");
    return false;
  }

  CallFrame* frame = &vm.frames[vm.frameCount++];

  frame->function = callee;
  frame->ip = function->chunk.code;
  frame->slots = vm.stackTop - argCount - 1;

  return true;
}
```

Next, create a wrapper for closure calls:

```c
static bool callClosure(ObjClosure* closure,
                        int argCount) {
  return call((Obj*)closure,
              closure->function,
              argCount);
}
```

And a wrapper for ordinary function calls:

```c
static bool callFunction(ObjFunction* function,
                         int argCount) {
  return call((Obj*)function,
              function,
              argCount);
}
```

Both types of functions still share
the same argument-count checks,
call-frame allocation, and stack layout.

The main difference is how the VM
obtains the underlying function object.

## 5. Update Bytecode and Constant Access

In Chapter 25, the VM accesses constants
through the ObjFunction owned by
the current closure.

Since an ordinary function might not
have a closure, this access must change.

In vm.c, the READ_CONSTANT macro can
use the new helper:

```c
#define READ_CONSTANT() \
  (getFrameFunction(frame)->chunk.constants.values[READ_BYTE()])
```

The helper returns the appropriate
ObjFunction regardless of whether the
current CallFrame contains an ordinary
function or a closure.

The other bytecode-reading macros can
continue using the existing instruction
pointer mechanism.

## 6. Update Debugging and Stack Traces

The VM also uses the current function
to display executed instructions and
report runtime errors.

In runtimeError(), the original Chapter 25
code accesses:

```c
ObjFunction* function = frame->closure->function;
```

I would replace it with:

```c
ObjFunction* function = getFrameFunction(frame);
```

This ensures that runtime errors still
display the correct function name and
source line for both callable types.

The instruction tracing code must
also retrieve the current chunk
through getFrameFunction().

For example:

```c
ObjFunction* function = getFrameFunction(frame);

disassembleInstruction(
    &function->chunk,
    (int)(frame->ip - function->chunk.code));
```

If the instruction-pointer optimization
from Chapter 24, Challenge 1 is also
installed, this debug expression should
use the cached local ip variable instead
of frame->ip.

## 7. Preserve Upvalue Access

Only functions with captured variables
will need to execute OP_GET_UPVALUE
and OP_SET_UPVALUE.

These functions are still wrapped
in ObjClosure objects.

Because CallFrame now stores an Obj*
instead of a dedicated closure pointer,
the VM must obtain the closure explicitly
when accessing upvalues.

For example, OP_GET_UPVALUE can use:

```c
case OP_GET_UPVALUE: {
  uint8_t slot = READ_BYTE();

  ObjClosure* closure =
      (ObjClosure*)frame->function;

  push(*closure->upvalues[slot]->location);
  break;
}
```

Similarly, OP_SET_UPVALUE can use:

```c
case OP_SET_UPVALUE: {
  uint8_t slot = READ_BYTE();

  ObjClosure* closure =
      (ObjClosure*)frame->function;

  *closure->upvalues[slot]->location = peek(0);
  break;
}
```

These instructions are only generated
for functions with upvalues.

The OP_CLOSURE instruction also needs to
access the enclosing closure when an
upvalue captures another upvalue.

For that case, the VM should cast
the current CallFrame's function field
to ObjClosure before reading its
upvalue array.

These changes preserve the variable
capture behavior introduced in Chapter 25.

## 8. Update the Initial Script Function

The compiler still returns an ObjFunction
for the top-level script.

Since the script itself does not need
to capture enclosing local variables,
it can be executed directly.

In interpret(), after compiling the script,
the setup can use:

```c
ObjFunction* function = compile(source);

if (function == NULL) {
  return INTERPRET_COMPILE_ERROR;
}

push(OBJ_VAL(function));
callFunction(function, 0);

return run();
```

The surrounding VM initialization and
error handling remain unchanged.

This avoids unnecessarily wrapping
the script's function in an ObjClosure.

## 9. Benchmark 1: Program Without Closures

The first benchmark measures a program
that does not capture variables.

Recursive Fibonacci is useful because
it involves many ordinary function calls.

```lox
fun fib(n) {
  if (n < 2) return n;

  return fib(n - 2) + fib(n - 1);
}

var start = clock();

print fib(30);

print clock() - start;
```

Expected Fibonacci result:

```text
832040
```

The last number printed by this program
is the measured CPU time.

The function fib() does not capture
variables from an enclosing function.

Under the proposed optimization,
it should therefore be represented
using ObjFunction without creating
an additional ObjClosure.

This test helps evaluate the overhead
of supporting both callable types.

## 10. Benchmark 2: Program That Creates Closures

The second benchmark creates many functions
and closures inside a loop.

This is adapted from the uploaded
suggested solution.

```lox
var start = clock();
var sum = 0;

for (var j = 0; j < 100000; j = j + 1) {
  fun outer(a, b, c) {
    fun inner() {
      return a + b + c;
    }

    return inner;
  }

  var closure = outer(j, j, j);
  sum = sum + closure();
}

print sum;
print clock() - start;
```

In this example, outer() does not capture
any variables from an enclosing function.

However, inner() captures the parameters
a, b, and c from outer().

Under the proposed design:

- outer() can be represented directly
  using ObjFunction.
- inner() needs an ObjClosure because
  it captures three variables.

This benchmark tests a situation
where both types of function objects
are used repeatedly.

It also helps investigate the effects
of avoiding unnecessary closure
allocations.

## 11. Benchmarking Method

To compare the two implementations fairly,
I would use:

1. The original Chapter 25 implementation,
   where every function gets an ObjClosure.

2. The modified implementation, where
   only functions with upvalues are wrapped.

Both implementations should use the same
compiler version, optimization settings,
hardware, and benchmark inputs.

Debug tracing should be disabled because
it adds work that could interfere with
execution-time measurements.

Each benchmark should be run multiple
times, with the results compared using
a consistent measurement method.

I would also include programs that
frequently call closures without
creating new closures each time.

This is important because closure
creation and closure invocation
exercise different parts of the VM.

## 12. Performance Analysis

Avoiding unnecessary ObjClosure allocations
could reduce work when executing function
declarations that do not capture variables.

However, the VM must now distinguish
between ObjFunction and ObjClosure
when processing function calls and
retrieving bytecode information.

This introduces additional conditional
logic into the implementation.

As a result, some workloads might
benefit from the optimization,
while others might not.

A benchmark that repeatedly creates
functions could behave differently
from one that mostly calls functions
created earlier.

I have not run these benchmarks
or collected timing measurements,
so I cannot determine which version
is faster or by how much.

## 13. How Should the Benchmarks Be Weighted?

I would not choose an implementation
based on only one benchmark.

Instead, I would consider how frequently
each operation occurs in realistic
Lox programs.

For example, most ordinary programs
may call functions far more often
than they create new closures.

If the optimization improves closure
creation but makes every function call
more complicated, that trade-off
might not be worthwhile.

On the other hand, if a program creates
many functions dynamically, avoiding
unnecessary allocations could be
more valuable.

I would give more weight to workloads
that represent how the interpreter
is actually expected to be used.

I would also consider memory usage
and implementation complexity,
not just execution time.

## 14. Is the Added Complexity Worth It?

The main advantage of this approach
is that functions without captured
variables do not need an extra
closure object.

This could reduce memory allocations
and simplify runtime representation
for those functions.

The disadvantage is that the VM
can no longer assume every callable
Lox function is an ObjClosure.

Several parts of the interpreter
must now handle two different
function representations.

This adds conditional logic and
increases the amount of code
that needs to be maintained.

Personally, I think the optimization
is interesting and worth testing,
but I would not automatically choose
it over the original design.

The original Chapter 25 approach
is simpler because every Lox
function call follows the same path.

Without measured performance results,
I would lean toward keeping the
simpler implementation unless
benchmarking demonstrates a
meaningful practical benefit.
